snippets.code:
.panel {
  display: flex;
  gap: 1rem;
}

explanation:
The `hidden` attribute doesn't have special rendering powers. It works because the browser's built-in (UA) stylesheet has a rule like `[hidden] { display: none; }`.

Normal (non-`!important`) author styles beat UA styles in the cascade regardless of specificity, so `.panel { display: flex }` wins and the panel is rendered. `getComputedStyle(panel).display` is `"flex"` even though `panel.hidden` is `true`.

The usual fix is a small reset in your own stylesheet:

```css
[hidden] {
  display: none !important;
}
```

Or scope it: `.panel[hidden] { display: none; }`. The same trap catches any component that sets `display` on the element itself.

